        { "de": "Befund", "article": "der", "plural": "die Befunde", "en": "finding; report", "emoji": "📋", "glossaryKey": "befund" },
        { "de": "Bett", "article": "das", "plural": "die Betten", "en": "bed", "emoji": "🛏️", "image": { "type": "svg", "id": "bed" }, "glossaryKey": "bett" }
      ],
      "text": {
        "title": "Ich hatte zu lange geschlafen",
        "de": "Ich hatte zu lange geschlafen. Vor der Operation hatte der Patient schon gegessen. Er war schon gekommen. Die Pflegekraft hatte das Fieber schon gemessen. Anna hatte den Befund schon geschrieben. Der Patient war im Bett geblieben. Dann kam der Arzt.",
        "en": "I had slept too long. Before the operation the patient had already eaten. He had already come. The nurse had already measured the fever. Anna had already written the report. The patient had stayed in bed. Then the doctor came."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar",
      "type": "grammar",
      "label": "Grammar",
      "title": "hatte or war",
      "practice": "choose",
      "heroTitle": "Ich hatte zu lange geschlafen",
      "heroImage": "clipboard",
      "story": [
        "The Plusquamperfekt marks what happened <b>before</b> another past event. <b>Ich hatte zu lange geschlafen. Vor der Operation hatte der Patient schon gegessen. Er war schon gekommen. Dann kam der Arzt.</b> kam is the later event. The sleeping, the meal and the arrival are earlier.",
        "Build it like the Perfekt. The participle does not change: geschlafen, gegessen, gemessen, geschrieben, gekommen, geblieben. A <b>haben</b> verb takes <b>hatte</b>. A <b>sein</b> verb takes <b>war</b>. schlafen, essen, messen, schreiben, trinken, geben and untersuchen take hatte. kommen, gehen, bleiben and aufstehen take war. untersuchen has no extra ge-: untersucht.",
        "The auxiliary is in <b>position 2</b>. The participle stays at the <b>end</b>. <b>Vor der Operation</b> is one time phrase in position 1, so the verb inverts: Vor der Operation <b>hatte</b> er schon gegessen. vor takes the dative, as in vor der Visite: vor <b>der</b> Operation. Not vor die Operation. The conjunction bevor, which sends its verb to the end, comes in a later chapter.",
        "ich and er share hatte and war. du adds -st: <b>hattest, warst</b>. wir, sie and Sie: <b>hatten, waren</b>. ihr adds -t: <b>hattet, wart</b>. Movement into the room is <b>ins Zimmer</b>: Er war ins Zimmer gegangen. Location is <b>im Bett</b>: Er war im Bett geblieben.",
        "Three pasts stay put. A spoken progress note still uses the Perfekt: hat gemessen, ist gekommen, hat gegessen. Those sentences are correct there. A state still has no participle: hatte Fieber, war müde. A modal stays konnte. aß and schlief are the narrative past of that event itself. Before the operation, the earlier meal is hatte gegessen.",
        "schon sits in the Mittelfeld: hatte schon gegessen, war schon gekommen. zu lange stays two words: hatte zu lange geschlafen."
      ],
